    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d243">

                <pb facs="0406" n="278" sameAs="frus:frus1928v01/pg278"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15Franco–British/16: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in
                        France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Herrick</hi>)</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1928-08-11T15:00:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">August 11,
                                1928—3 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>255. For Marriner. Reference to Atherton’s telegram No. 184, from London, August
                    10, 5 p.m., regarding your conversation with Lord Cushendun. The President
                    desires that neither I nor any of our Embassies discuss further the Anglo-French
                    agreement on subject of naval disarmament, and nothing will be done here in
                    regard to it until the President returns to Washington.<anchor n="45"
                        xml:id="d243-f1"/> Transmit copy to our Embassies London and Brussels.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Kellogg</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d243-f1" n="45" xml:id="d243-f1-content">
                <p>This sentence was also telegraphed as an instruction to the Embassies in Germany
                    (No. 89), Italy (No. 97), and Japan on August 22.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
